  "live_records": {
    "q-162233": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162233",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{z/2}{\\int }_{x}^{z+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3-z}}{\\int }_{x}^{z+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type III regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{z}{2},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le z+1\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3-z},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le z+1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{x}^{z+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/b9d4ce5d2594c8d857cbc456b1e9a098deade7f012ad6b90edc17f06b7ffde13.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{z}{2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,2\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3-z}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{z}{2}\\,⟹\\,z=2x\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3-z}\\,⟹\\,z=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2x\\le z\\le -{x}^{2}+3\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/e89d26f7f129901b2ff004cac31c0d643c86fff5cf38ef559311131ac4122e0e.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{-{x}^{2}+3}{\\int }_{x}^{z+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
