    "q-162251": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162251",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/62377f3e744c5767927f9591b8c6e3335a8036cad5fe9c62f52c041b2db2bac8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/05de07930f6cacfd0a3a21045a95628891965bb69d5df1bef4dbeeab9e480b97.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[x]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
