    "q-162333": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162333",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{p}^{0}{\\int }_{q}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,y)}^{{u}_{4}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,x\\le z\\le {y}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/a38afc63496b487803590ccd57e2ab92ad4061524007e8bb4fb6277ff952dde2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/9bbaf6d9ce11ecbd988954365e9693f7f3f175c1dd0f944cc85b73773beca047.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\le x\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\le x\\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2y+4}}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
